CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*invalid implicit conversion from '<<type:.*'.*$
--
Verifies that a variable template whose initializer body fails to
type-check (because it depends on an unmodelled libstdc++ helper)
falls through to a typed `symbol_exprt` at use sites instead of
inlining a corrupted `cpp_name` body.  The libstdc++ pattern this
mirrors is `is_trivially_destructible_v<T>` whose body
`is_trivially_destructible<T>::value` cascades through
`__is_destructible_safe<T>` and friends; for non-trivial class types
like `std::basic_string` CBMC's typechecker can't always evaluate
the chain to a bool constant.

Before the fix the use site of the broken variable template
produced
  invalid implicit conversion from '<<type:>>' to 'bool'
when the value was used as a non-type bool template argument.
